compliant=false against `rules/rule-authoring.md`, with four breaches named, all four inside the
single numbered clause.

First, the clause HEADING states an obligation — "Runs BEFORE The Cutover, Never After It" —
while the BODY hedges throughout: should generally, are encouraged to move toward, usually less
helpful, tends to be worth avoiding. Its closing sentence then returns the exact case the
heading forbids, allowing the cutover to run first with the warm pass following under live
traffic whenever the window is tight. A rule that hands back its own forbidden case teaches the
exception, not the rule.

Second, the `**BLOCKED rationalizations:**` heading is present and holds six ABSTRACT CATEGORY
LABELS — "Optimism bias regarding natural warm-up intervals", "Deploy-window pressure effects" —
instead of the literal first-person sentences a session emits. No session ever writes those
words, so the corpus traps nothing.

Third, the two arms of the worked block are the SAME LINE. Both read "warm the cache against the
new build, then cut traffic over to it" and both quote the same example; they are separated only
by the trailing adverbs "carefully" and "carelessly". The contrast slot is filled and carries no
contrast, so the block cannot teach the ordering it exists to teach.

Fourth, the justification runs well past the two-sentence cap, wandering through traffic
profile, working-set size, cacheable-route ratio and key-scheme addressability before closing on
a recommendation to consider the ordering alongside those — which also softens the obligation a
second time.

